# Target C: nine-no-root k=6 p=5 support-nullity compression

Date: 2026-10-04
Base main: d432d18a14cfeccbb7e47c51960847ecc33351f2
Dependency branch: worker-b/nine-noroot-k6-p5-quotient-basis-cover-2026-10-04
Status: proof-level reduction plus executed exact finite support census. C-II and Level I remain open.

Assume the surviving nine-no-root branch

    k=6, D=-1, d_root=1, p=5,

with rank-three coefficient space P and generalized weights
(d1,d2,d3)(P)>=(2,4,5).

The singleton-generator reduction leaves exactly two selected incomplete-root
profiles:

1. three singleton blocks and one pair block;
2. five singleton blocks.

It also proves there is no touched actual-empty block, every selected incomplete
block is touched, every other touched block is complete, and the touched
complete-root nullity is at most one.

Let F be the set of distinct tetrahedral four-supports used by the nine no-root
cycles, let m=|F|, and let T be the union of all triangular faces of supports in
F. Since one tetrahedral support has only three undirected four-cycle
orientations, nine distinct no-root rows force m>=3.

For a triangle set X write

    nu(X)=|X|-rank H(X),

where H(X) is the oriented triangle-boundary matrix in the 21 edge coordinates
of K_7.

## 1. Full touched-root nullity bound

Let I be the selected incomplete touched root blocks and C_touch=T\I. There are
no touched empty blocks, so this is a partition of T.

Because d_root=1,

    |C_touch|-rank H(C_touch) <= 1.

Since H(C_touch) is a row submatrix of H(T),

    rank H(T) >= rank H(C_touch)
              >= |T|-|I|-1.

Therefore

    nu(T) <= |I|+1.                                      (1)

Consequently:

- in the three-singleton-plus-pair profile, |I|=4 and nu(T)<=5;
- in the five-singleton profile, |I|=5 and nu(T)<=6.

This uses the full touched triangle-row nullity, not only the span of
tetrahedron-boundary dependencies.

## 2. Exact S_7 support census

I executed an exact orbit enumeration of tetrahedral support families on seven
vertices. The search starts from all three-support S_7 orbits and extends each
retained representative by one tetrahedron. The pruning condition is nu(T)<=6.
This is exhaustive because triangle-row nullity is monotone under adding rows.

For each family the verifier forms T and computes rank H(T) modulo the prime
1000003. The modular rank equals the rational rank: every triangle row has
Euclidean norm sqrt(3), every square minor has order at most 21, and Hadamard
gives

    |det| <= 3^(21/2) < 1000003.

Hence a nonzero integer minor cannot vanish modulo the checked prime.

The exact retained orbit census is

    m=3: 10 orbits, nullity distribution {3:10}
    m=4: 38 orbits, nullity distribution {4:35,5:3}
    m=5: 135 orbits, nullity distribution {4:1,5:107,6:27}
    m=6: 302 orbits, nullity distribution {5:2,6:300}
    m=7: 10 orbits, nullity distribution {6:10}
    m=8: 0 orbits.

Thus the profile-specific consequences are:

### Three singletons plus one pair

The bound nu(T)<=5 gives

    3 <= m <= 6.                                         (2)

At m=6 there are exactly two S_7 support orbits. They are the five tetrahedral
facets on a common five-vertex set plus one extra tetrahedron, with the extra
tetrahedron meeting that five-set in respectively three or two vertices.

### Five singletons

The bound nu(T)<=6 gives

    3 <= m <= 7.                                         (3)

At m=7 there are exactly ten S_7 support orbits, all with nu(T)=6. No
eight-support family survives.

Since the nine cycle multiplicities are positive and at most three, every m=7
candidate has multiplicity profile either

    (3,1,1,1,1,1,1)

or

    (2,2,1,1,1,1,1).

This reduces the top support layer of the five-singleton profile to twenty
support/multiplicity types before singleton placement, cycle orientation,
generalized-weight, quotient-basis-cover, and external relation-rank tests.

